        <Options xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false" greenerHomesTab="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air leakage can account for a large portion of the heating costs in a home, especially with older homes such as this were air barriers were not installed. Since it is far too expensive to remove the finishing material and install an fully sealed air barrier, it is important to seal areas of air leakage and use the finishing material as the air barrier.                                             During the evaluation the evaluator hooked up a blower door to one of the exterior doors and depressurized the house. This removes air out of the home at a high speed causing air to be pulled in through any holes or gaps in the homes building envelope, as would happen naturally with a strong wind blowing against the home. A thermal camera was then used to see these areas of air leakage and can be seeen on the thermal pictures that were emailed to you by your evaluator. The blue areas in these pictures show cold, exterior air entering the home, these are areas that should be sealed. These area are also listed below, with ideas on how they can be sealed, if unsure how to do the work yourself, consult a qualified contractor or air sealing expert.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   - There was a lot of air coming in around the edges of your attic hatch, this should be properly insulated with 8 inches of foam insulation, the edges of the hatch should be properly weatherstripped, latches should be installed to pull the hatch down tight against the weatherstripping and the trim around the hatch should be caulked.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- Since there is no air barrier in the attic a lot of air blows through penetrations through the ceiling, therefore it is important to seal these penetrations wherever possibe, this is best done from inside the attic. Client should caulk or spray foam around any light fixtures, around the bathroom vent, plumbing vent stack and any partition walls where they go through the ceiling. The client should also seal where the chimney goes through the ceiling, however this should be done safely with high heat insulation, a attic flashing kit and high heat caukling.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- It is important that any exhaust vent cover is working properly, both to keep cold air out and to keep pests out. Client is advised to replace any damage vent covers and/or change them for ones that seal much better than old flapper vents, such as ball dampers.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- It is common with older houses to get cold air coming in around the edges of the walls where they meet the floor. Ideally the baseboard trim should be removed and the gap between the wall and floor should be sealed with a flexible caulking or low expansion spray foam for larger gaps. If trim is not removed, caulk the edges of the trim where it meets the walls and floor. The inspector also noted air coming in around where the side baseboard trim of the stairs meets the wall, this should be cauliked. Caution: spray foam should always be allowed to dry before trim is re-installed as it will expand several times it's original size.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- The client is adivsed to seal around all windows and replace weatherstripping periodically. Ideally for older windows were spray foam was never used, the trim should be reomoved and low expansion spray foam should be installed in the gaps between the wall and window frame. If trim is not removed or after it is spray faomed and re-installed,  proper caulking should be used to seal where the window itself meets the interior wood frame and caulking should be used to seal the inside of the trim and where the trim meets the wall. Also remember to have the exterior of the windows caulked as part of routine maintenance to prevent water infiltration around the window.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A; - Older wood doors are not very energy efficient and should ideally be replaced with new insulated doors. However if the old doors are staying, the client is advised to properly weatherstrip around the entire door and the trim should be sealed in the same way as the windows. Also any holes in the door such as mail slots should be sealed.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- Any outlets or switches around the home, especially on the exterior walls, should have the faceplate removed, a faom gasket installed and child proof plugs installed in any of the outlets that are not being used. &#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- The inspector noted a lot of air coming in around the propane insert, this is usually from between the newer metal liner and the original clay liner. This can be difficult to properly seal as the unit needs to be pulled out and proper high temperature insulation and a high temperature sealant installed between the new and nold liner. If this is not done suggest getting high heat insulation and stuffing it around all the edges of the insert to reduce air flow.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A; - The inspector noted air coming in around the kitchen cupboards, especially the top ones on the back wall. Suggest opening the cupboards and sealing any holes as well as the edges of the cupboards where they meet the wall.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;- The basement headers are an area of significant air infiltration as stack effect will cause cold air to be pushed in through any gaps in the headers, therefore it is important that these are insulated and sealed properly to reduce this infiltration. Ideally spray foam should be used as this will seal all small gaps and act as a vapour/air barrier as well. If spray foam is not used batt insulation can be put into the headers, as much as can fit without compressing it, then snug fitting pieces of ridgid styrofaom insulation should be installed overtop the batt fiberglass and spray faom used around the edges to seal it in place. Also any penetrations through the headers with pipes or electrical should be sealed with spray foam or caulking. &#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;If the client is unsure of how to perform any of the air sealing, consult with your energy evaluator ro a qualified contractor.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The inspector noticed that there did not seem to be any insulation in the wall at the back of the kitchen, this should be insulated with blown in insulation, unless the kitchen is to be gutted, then a batt insulation can be used. </mainWalls>
                <foundation>The inspector noticed that there were some areas of the basement that were insulated, however there did not appear to be a proper moisture barrier against the concrete wall and some of the insulation and framing was getting wet. Suggest removing the wood paneling and any wet building material before having the walls properly waterproofed and insulated. If concerned, suggest consulting a qualified foundation specialist. Also the headers in the basement should be properly insulated an sealed.</foundation>
                <windows>The inspector noted moisture between the panes of the front living room window, this reduces the energy efficiency of the window. The side older slider windows leak quite badly and are not very energy efficient. Suggest replacing these three windows with new Energy Star windows.</windows>
                <doors>The older wood doors do not insulate or seal well. Suggest replacing with insulated doors that have proper seals.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>If the hot water tank is being replaced, suggest considering a more efficient heat pump hot water heater as an energy upgrade.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Air-source heat pumps, which include air-to-air and air-to-water heat pumps, extract heat in the air from outside and transfer it to a distribution system in the house during the heating season.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;Different terminology is used for the efficiency ratings of heat pumps.   For example, air-source heat pumps have seasonal heating and cooling ratings. The heating rating is the Heating Seasonal Performance Factor (HSPF), while the cooling rating is the Seasonal Energy Efficiency Ratio (SEER). However, in the manufacturers' catalogues you may still see Coefficient of Performance (COP) or Energy Efficiency Rating (EER) ratings. COP is used to rate cooling or heating efficiencies and EER only rates the cooling efficiency. The higher the rating, the more energy efficient is the heat pump. ENERGY STAR® qualified heat pumps are among the most energy efficient in the marketplace.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;An air-source heat pump consists of a matched condenser coil (outdoor unit comprising a condenser coil, compressor and cooling fan) and an indoor evaporator coil (typically located with the furnace).  Many manufacturers voluntarily submit their products for testing by the Air Conditioning, Heating and Refrigeration Institute (AHRI) to assure consumers that their energy efficiency claims have been verified by an independent, third-party source. However, the AHRI does not certify individual indoor or outdoor coils.  The AHRI only certifies matched indoor and outdoor coils which work together to achieve the given SEER/HSPF rating for the heat pump system.  Note that air-source heat pumps can also be sold together with a furnace or fan-coil.  In these cases the AHRI number will refer to a furnace or fan-coil in addition to the indoor and outdoor coils.&#x0D;&#x0A;&#x0D;&#x0A;Where you have a new indoor compressor unit, you should talk to Lennox to find exterior heat pump units that will match with this internal coil.. You should also consult with efficiency ns or your energy evaluator to ensure it will qualify for a rebate or financing.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>A drain water heat recovery system will extract heat from grey water and use it to pre heat the water going into the hot water tank. This is a passive system that does not need maintenance once it is installed. If you are replacing the older cast iron drain line with a new abs line this would be the ideal time to install one of these systems.</Renewables>
            </RURComments>
            <GreenerHomes basementSlabInsulated="false" moistureProofCrawlSpace="false" waterproofing="false" roofingMembrane="false" minR10ContinuousExposedFloors="false" backwaterValve="false" sumpPump="false" smartThermostats="false" remoteCommunities="false" evaluationCost="" />
        </Options>
